    <l n="1">§. 2. Atque ex his quidem evidenter</l>
    <l n="2">deducitur, fidera esse majora, quam vi-</l>
    <l n="3">sui apparent: at quoufque majora sint,</l>
    <l n="4">proprium est Astronomis &amp; Opticis in-</l>
    <l n="5">vestigare. Hi vero assumpta Sphærica</l>
    <l n="6">astrorum figura, duo alia requirunt</l>
    <l n="7">suppolita. 1. cognitionem distantiæ</l>
    <l n="8">stellarum, 2. cognitionem apparentis</l>
    <l n="9">magnitudinis stellarum. Nam tunc in-</l>
    <l n="10">venitigant principio diametrum veram.</l>
    <l n="11">Ex ea, proportione Archimedea, ut 7.</l>
    <l n="12">ad 22. ita diameter ad Peripheriam, Pe-</l>
    <l n="13">ripheria investigatur. Porro multipli-</l>
    <l n="14">catione Peripheriæ &amp; Diametri oritur</l>
    <l n="15">superficiei quantitas convexa. Et ulti-</l>
    <l n="16">mo ducendo semidiametrum in ter-</l>
    <l n="17">tiam areæ convexæ partem producitur</l>
    <l n="18">soliditas sphærici corporis.</l>
    <l n="19" para="true">§. 3. De his videantur Mathematici</l>
    <l n="20">ipsi, nobis hæc quæstio furgit: <i>An ea,</i>
    </l>
    <l n="21">
      <i>qua Mathematici de fiderum magnitudi-</i>
    </l>
    <l n="22">
      <i>ne tradunt, sint omnino certa?</i> R. Non</l>
    <l n="23">quidem in dubium voco, quæ de pro-</l>
    <l n="24">portione mutua &amp; calculo in investi-</l>
    <l n="25">gatione magnitudinis tradunt: atta-</l>
    <l n="26">men incertam esse &amp; non præcise co-</l>
    <l n="27">gnitam fiderum magnitudinem inde</l>
    <l n="28">colligo. 1. quia, ipsis Mathematicis</l>
    <l n="29">prudentioribus fatentibus, distantiæ</l>
    <l n="30">fiderum non sunt exacte cognitæ. Et pone</l>
